+# ADR-0046: PDF rendering with headless Chromium via `playwright-core`
+
+## Status
+
+Proposed (claude, T-0041, 2026-11-09). Category `architecture`, `human_gate: no`: becomes
+**Accepted on green CI + cross-review by codex** (write-adr skill step 5). The reviewer should
+also confirm the sandbox rules below; if they judge the browser sandbox a security-model change,
+re-classify to `security` (human gate) in a superseding ADR rather than editing this one.
+
+## Context and problem statement
+
+Design §7.1 fixes the PDF technology: "HTML templates rendered by headless Chromium — same
+template engine as email and previews". Design §4.3 makes every billing format a **template
+record** (PDF layout, email template, …) that tenants clone and adjust. T-0041 must turn the
+posted sales invoice (T-0040), the adjustment note (T-0045) and the customer statement (T-0049)
+into PDFs that meet the AU tax-invoice and adjustment-note content rules (RQ-0132 Q1–Q2, from
+RQ-0009 and RQ-0114) and are provably reproducible.
+
+Running a browser in the API/worker at **runtime** is a new runtime dependency (AGENTS.md §4,
+design §10.6), so it needs this ADR. Until now Playwright was a dev dependency only
+(`@playwright/test` 1.63.0, e2e and evidence; ADR-0015).
+
+Observed facts (RQ-0132 S5–S7): `playwright-core` 1.63.0 already resolves in the lockfile (it is
+what `@playwright/test` runs on); it downloads no browser; `page.pdf()` is Chromium-only; two
+renders differ only in `/CreationDate` and `/ModDate`; Liberation Sans embeds as an extractable
+Type0 font while a variable font (Inter) becomes Type3 glyph fonts.
+
+## Decision drivers
+
+- Design §7.1: one HTML template engine for PDF, email and preview; headless Chromium named.
+- Determinism and auditability: a PDF must be reproducible and hashable (directive 8, RQ-0132 Q5).
+- Tenant content (names, footer, logo) is untrusted input to a browser: no script execution, no
+  network fetch (design §6, RQ-0132 Q6).
+- Minimise new supply-chain surface: prefer a package already pinned and reviewed in the repo.
+- Self-host (design §8.4 Docker Compose) and agent sandboxes: the Chromium binary location differs
+  per environment and must be configuration.
+
+## Considered options
+
+1. **`playwright-core` + system/pinned Chromium** — reuse the version the test suite pins.
+2. **`puppeteer-core` + Chromium** — the other mainstream CDP driver.
+3. **A JavaScript PDF library (`pdfkit` / `pdf-lib`)** — draw the PDF directly, no browser.
+4. **An external rendering service (Gotenberg container)** — HTTP API in front of Chromium.
+
+### Option 1 — playwright-core
+
+- Good, because it is already in the lockfile at 1.63.0 (same version as `@playwright/test`), so
+  the browser build the CI installs (`playwright install --with-deps chromium`) matches.
+- Good, because `launch({ executablePath })`, `newContext({ javaScriptEnabled: false })`,
+  `page.route()` and `page.pdf({ tagged: true })` cover the sandbox and accessibility needs.
+- Good, because the HTML renderer is exactly the one the e2e evidence screenshots use.
+- Bad, because Chromium is a large runtime binary (~150 MB) and must be patched with the image.
+- Bad, because a browser process per render is slow (~0.3–1 s); batch runs need a pooled browser.
+
+### Option 2 — puppeteer-core
+
+- Good, because it is purpose-built for headless Chrome and widely used for PDFs.
+- Bad, because it is a second driver and a new dependency tree, for the same Chromium.
+
+### Option 3 — pdfkit / pdf-lib
+
+- Good, because no browser binary; small and fast.
+- Bad, because it is a second layout engine: the PDF and the email/preview HTML would diverge,
+  contrary to design §7.1, and tenants could not adjust layout as data.
+
+### Option 4 — Gotenberg service
+
+- Good, because it isolates the browser in its own container.
+- Bad, because it adds a service to self-host (design §8.4 single VM), a network hop and an
+  unpinned API surface; the same isolation is achievable in-process with JS off and network
+  aborted. Can be revisited for scale.
+
+## Decision outcome
+
+Chosen option: **Option 1 — `playwright-core` 1.63.0 driving headless Chromium**, because it
+satisfies design §7.1 with the package and browser build the repo already pins, and the sandbox
+and determinism rules below are enforceable in code and tests.
+
+Rules (checkable):
+
+1. Only `packages/modules/billing/src/pdf-renderer.ts` imports `playwright-core` at runtime.
+   Its version is pinned exactly and equal to `@playwright/test`'s.
+2. The Chromium binary is configuration: `LF_CHROMIUM_PATH`, default `/opt/pw-browsers/chromium`;
+   a missing binary is a clear `ChromiumUnavailableError`, never a silent fallback or download.
+3. Every render runs in a fresh browser context with **JavaScript disabled** and **every network
+   request aborted** (`page.route('**/*', abort)`); images come only from `data:` URLs that the
+   template builds from stored logo bytes (PNG, JPEG, SVG; ≤ 256 KiB).
+4. Templates are pure functions of (document model, billing format) returning HTML; every
+   interpolated value is HTML-escaped; no arithmetic on money in templates (amounts are decimal
+   strings from the AR module, formatted with `Money` — never `number`).
+5. Output is deterministic: `/CreationDate` and `/ModDate` are set to the document date at the
+   same byte length, so identical inputs give identical bytes on the same Chromium build and fonts;
+   the SHA-256 is returned with the bytes. Font stack starts with **Liberation Sans** (not a
+   variable font).
+6. PDFs are tagged (`tagged: true`) A4 with backgrounds printed.
+
+## Consequences
+
+### Good
+
+- One template engine for PDF, email (T-0042) and the UI preview (T-0083).
+- Reproducible PDFs: tests snapshot the HTML and assert byte-identical re-renders and text content.
+- Tenant templates cannot execute code or reach the network.
+
+### Bad / accepted trade-offs
+
+- The API/worker image must ship Chromium and `fonts-liberation`; the self-host image grows.
+- Byte-identity holds per Chromium build and font set; a browser upgrade changes hashes (the HTML
+  snapshot and text assertions still hold). Stored hashes describe the stored bytes, not a
+  re-render on a newer build.
+- Rendering is slow relative to a PDF library; batch billing (T-0043) should reuse one browser
+  (`DocumentRenderer` keeps one browser open across renders and is closed explicitly).
+
+### Follow-ups
+
+- T-0180: persist renditions (attachment store, link from the invoice and `LF_Invoice__c` PDF link).
+- Infra: add Chromium + `fonts-liberation` to the worker/API container image (tracked on T-0180).
+
+## Compliance and verification
+
+| Check | Where | Gate |
+|---|---|---|
+| GP-001 invoice PDF text contains "Tax invoice", the number, the ABN, GST 125.00, total 1,375.00 | `packages/modules/billing/src/invoice-pdf.snapshot.db.test.ts` | `pnpm test:db` (verify gate 2) |
+| HTML is byte-equal to the committed snapshot; re-render gives identical PDF bytes and SHA-256 | `packages/modules/billing/src/documents.snapshot.test.ts` | `pnpm test` (verify gate 2) |
+| JS disabled and network aborted: a template with `<script>` and an external `<img>` makes no request and runs nothing | `packages/modules/billing/src/pdf-renderer.test.ts` | `pnpm test` |
+| Every interpolated value escaped | `packages/modules/billing/src/html.test.ts` | `pnpm test` |
+| Missing Chromium binary → `ChromiumUnavailableError` | `packages/modules/billing/src/pdf-renderer.test.ts` | `pnpm test` |
+| Only the renderer imports playwright-core at runtime | `grep -rn "playwright-core" packages apps --include=*.ts` in review checklist | cross-review |
+
